Figuratively —'''2.''' Belonging to a period corresponding to autumn in the year; hence, past the middle stage of life: as, “an ''autumnal'' matron,” ''Hawthorne.''—'''Autumnal equinox''', {{smaller|the time when the sun crosses the equator as he proceeds southward. This happens about the 22d of September. See ''equinox.''}}—'''Autumnal plumage''', {{smaller|in ''ornith.'', the plumage acquired by a bird after the first molt, when that in which the bird leaves the nest is exchanged for another; the plumage of an annotine; also, that subsequently acquired each autumn by such birds as molt at that season as well as in spring, or have what is termed the double molt.}}—'''Autumnal signs''', {{smaller|the signs Libra, Scorpio, and Sagittarius, through which the sun passes during the autumn, astronomically considered.}}

'''II.''' ''n.'' A plant that flowers in autumn.

'''autumn-bells''' (â′tum-belz), ''n.'' A name given to a European gentian, ''Gentiana Pneumonanthe'', from its bell-shaped flowers and their season of opening.

'''autumnian''' (â-tum′ni-a̤n), ''a.'' [< ''autumn'' + ''-ian.''] Autumnal. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>Methinks already
I grasp best part of the ''autumnian'' blessing.</poem>
{{right|''Middleton'', Michaelmas Term, Ind.}}}}

'''autumnity''' (â-tum′ni-ti), ''n.'' [< L. ''autumnitas'', the season of autumn, harvest, < ''autumnus'', autumn.] The season of autumn; quality or condition characteristic of autumn. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|Draughts of sweet ''autumnity.'' {{right|''Bp. Hall'', Satires, iii. 1.}}}}

